< s=S(),Yt=c("p"),Yt.textContent="NEBaseChanger uses primer melting temperature (Tm) when determining 
< how long a primer should be. For substitutions that span a mutation region greater than 6 nucleotide
< s, the 5' end of the primer is anchored in the center of the region and extended in the 3' direction
<  until a desired minimum Tm is achieved. For fewer than 6 substitutions, the center of the primer is
<  anchored to the nucleotide region and extended in both the 5' and 3' directions so that there are a
< t least 10 nucleotides that are 100% complementary on both sides of the mutation region and until a 
< desired minimum Tm is achieved. For deletions, the primer is designed by anchoring its 5' end to the
<  nucleotide adjacent to the selected mutation region and extending in the 3' direction until a desir
< ed minimum Tm is achieved. For insertions, the primer is designed by anchoring its 5' end to the las
< t nucleotide in the selected mutation region and extending in the 3' direction until a desired minim
< um Tm is achieved. Insertions are either added to the 5' end of one primer (6 or fewer nucleotides) 
< or both primers (more than 6 nucleotides). Tm\u2019s are calculated using nearest neighbor thermodyn
< amics data for matched and internal mismatched bases and corrected for buffer composition [see refer
< ences below]. Tms are calculated assuming the recommended buffer composition for Hot Start High-Fide
< lity DNA Polymerase, and the preferred primer concentration (500nM). An annealing temperature (Ta) f
< or the PCR is calculated in addition to the Tm, optimized for Q5 DNA polymerase.",cl=S(),ls=c("block
