> tContent="PRIMER CALCULATIONS",qs=S(),Ql=c("p"),Ql.textContent="NEBaseChanger uses primer melting te
> mperature (Tm) when determining how long a primer should be. For substitutions that span a mutation 
> region greater than 6 nucleotides, the 5' end of the primer is anchored in the center of the region 
> and extended in the 3' direction until a desired minimum Tm is achieved. For fewer than 6 substituti
> ons, the center of the primer is anchored to the nucleotide region and extended in both the 5' and 3
> ' directions so that there are at least 10 nucleotides that are 100% complementary on both sides of 
> the mutation region and until a desired minimum Tm is achieved. For deletions, the primer is designe
> d by anchoring its 5' end to the nucleotide adjacent to the selected mutation region and extending i
> n the 3' direction until a desired minimum Tm is achieved. For insertions, the primer is designed by
>  anchoring its 5' end to the last nucleotide in the selected mutation region and extending in the 3'
>  direction until a desired minimum Tm is achieved. Insertions are either added to the 5' end of one 
> primer (6 or fewer nucleotides) or both primers (more than 6 nucleotides). Tm\u2019s are calculated 
> using nearest neighbor thermodynamics data for matched and internal mismatched bases and corrected f
> or buffer composition [see references below]. Tms are calculated assuming the recommended buffer com
> position for Hot Start High-Fidelity DNA Polymerase, and the preferred primer concentration (500nM).
>  An annealing temperature (Ta) for the PCR is calculated in addition to the Tm, optimized for Q5 DNA
>  polymerase.",Vs=S(),Al=c("blockquote"),Al.innerHTML=`<ul><li>SantaLucia J Jr. 1998. <em>A unified v
> iew of polymer, dumbbell, and olignucleotide DNA nearest-neighbor thermodynamics.</em> Proc. Natl. A
> cad. Sci. USA <strong>95</strong>:1460\u201365</li> 	<li>Allawi HT, SantaLucia J Jr. 1997. <em>Th
> ermodynamics and NMR of internal G\u2212T misatches in DNA.</em> Biochemistry <strong>36</strong>:10
> 581\u201394</li> 	<li>Allawi HT, SantaLucia J Jr. 1998. <em>Nearest-neighbor thermodynamics of
>  internal A-C mismatches in DNA: sequence dependence and pH effects.</em> Biochemistry <strong>37</s
> trong>:9435\u201344</li> 	<li>Allawi HT, SantaLucia J Jr. 1998. <em>Nearest-neighbor thermodyn
> amics parameters for internal GA mismatches in DNA.</em> Biochemistry <strong>37</strong>:2170\u2013
> 79</li> 	<li>Allawi HT, SantaLucia J Jr. 1998. <em>Thermodynamics of internal C-T mismatches 
> in DNA.</em> Nucleic Acids Res. <strong>26</strong>:2694\u2013701</li> 	<li>SantaLucia, J Jr., Hicks
> , D. 2004. <em>The thermodynamics of DNA structural motifs.</em> Annu. Rev. Biophys. Biomol. Struct.
>  <strong>33</strong>:415\u201340.</li> 	<li>Peyret N, Seneviratne PA, Allawi HT, SantaLucia J Jr. 19
> 99. <em>Nearest-neighbor thermodynamics and NMR of DNA sequences with internal A-A, C-C, G-G, and T-
> T mismatches.</em> Biochemistry <strong>38</strong>:3468\u201377</li> 	<li>Bommarito S, Peyret N, S
> antaLucia J Jr. 2000. <em>Thermodynamic parameters for DNA sequences with dangling ends.</em> Nuclei
> c Acids Res. <strong>28</strong>:1929\u201334</li> 	<li>Owczarzy, R, You, Y, Moreira, B, Manthey
